<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Isle de France</h1><p class="st-meta">1715 CE – 1810 CE · capital Port Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French Mauritius, a naval base and plantation colony that threatened British India until its capture in 1810.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, abandoned by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-mauritius">Dutch Mauritius</a> in 1710, in 1715 and renamed it Isle de France. Settlers arrived from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Ile Bourbon</a> in 1721, and under governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-de-la-bourdonnais">Mahe de La Bourdonnais</a> from 1735 the colony was developed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> built as a capital and naval harbour.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Run first by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> and from 1767 by the crown, the colony relied on enslaved labour from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and food production. The intendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre">Pierre Poivre</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-seewoosagur-ramgoolam-botanical-garden">Pamplemousses garden</a>. During wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, corsairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-surcouf">Robert Surcouf</a> raided British shipping from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces captured the island in 1810, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1814 confirmed its cession along with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-seychelles">Seychelles</a>. Renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, it kept French law and language, and gained independence in 1968.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/isle-de-france.jpg" alt="Isle de France" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: David McSpadden from Daly City, United States · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eiffel_Tower_from_the_Tour_Montparnasse_3,_Paris_May_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1715 CE – 1810 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahe de La Bourdonnais, Pierre Poivre, Charles Decaen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Mauritian Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, Seychelles</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ile de France, French Mauritius</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=isle-de-france" aria-label="Isle de France on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6230 2924 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Isle de France lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6550 3180L5054 728M6550 3180L5054 728M6550 3180L6550 3180M6550 3180L6538 2607"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M6550 3180h0M6538 2607h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6550 3180h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6550 3180h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Louis. <a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=isle-de-france">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
